package forloop;

public class KuvvetTablosu {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Tablo basligini yazdir
        System.out.printf("%8s %8s %8s %8s %8s %8s%n", "Sayi", "^1", "^2", "^3", "^4", "^5");
        // Baslik altina bir ayirac cizgisi ekle
        System.out.println("-------------------------------------------------------");

        // 1'den 10'a kadar olan sayilar icin dongu
        for (int sayi = 1; sayi <= 10; sayi++) {
            // Sayiyi yazdir
            System.out.printf("%9d", sayi);
            // 1'den 5. kuvvete kadar olan degerleri hesaplamak icin ic dongu
            for (int kuvvet = 1; kuvvet <= 5; kuvvet++) {
                // Kuvvet degerini hesapla ve yazdir
                System.out.printf("%9d", (int) Math.pow(sayi, kuvvet));
            }
            // Her sayinin hesaplamasi tamamlandiktan sonra bir alt satira gec
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}

/**
 * Base class for any node implementation which contains a generic type
 * variable.
 *
 * All known subclasses: {@link TreeNode}, {@link SimpleNode}.
 *
 * @param <E> The type of the data held in the Node.
 *
 * @author <a href="https://github.com/aitorfi">aitorfi</a>
 */
public abstract class Node<E> {

    /**
     * Generic type data stored in the Node.
     */
    private E data;

    /**
     * Empty constructor.
     */
    public Node() {
    }

    /**
     * Initializes the Nodes' data.
     *
     * @param data Value to which data will be initialized.
     */
    public Node(E data) {
        this.data = data;
    }

    public E getData() {
        return data;
    }

    public void setData(E data) {
        this.data = data;
    }
}

/**
 * This class provides methods to find all combinations of integers from 0 to n-1
 * of a specified length k using backtracking.
 * 
 * <p><b>Problem Description:</b><br>
 * Given two integers n and k, return all possible combinations of k numbers chosen
 * from the range [0, 1, 2, ..., n-1]. The order of combinations in the output
 * does not matter, and each combination should be in non-decreasing order.
 * 
 * <p><b>Example Usage:</b></p>
 * <pre>
 * // Find all combinations of 2 numbers from {0, 1, 2, 3}
 * List&lt;List&lt;Integer&gt;&gt; result = ArrayCombination.combination(4, 2);
 * // Result: [[0,1], [0,2], [0,3], [1,2], [1,3], [2,3]]
 * 
 * // Find all combinations of 1 number from {0, 1, 2}
 * result = ArrayCombination.combination(3, 1);
 * // Result: [[0], [1], [2]]
 * </pre>
 * 
 * <p><b>Algorithm Explanation:</b><br>
 * This implementation uses backtracking to generate all possible combinations:
 * <ul>
 *   <li>At each step, we choose a number from the current start position to n-1</li>
 *   <li>Add it to the current combination</li>
 *   <li>Recursively build the remainder of the combination with numbers greater than the chosen one</li>
 *   <li>Backtrack by removing the last element to try other possibilities</li>
 * </ul>
 * 
 * <p><b>Time Complexity:</b> O(C(n, k) × k) - where C(n,k) is the binomial coefficient,
 * and we need O(k) to copy each combination.</p>
 * 
 * <p><b>Space Complexity:</b> O(k) for the recursion stack + O(C(n,k) × k) for storing
 * all combinations in the result list.</p>
 * 
 * @see <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combination">Combination (Wikipedia)</a>
 * @see <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backtracking">Backtracking (Wikipedia)</a>
 */
public final class ArrayCombination {

    // Private constructor to prevent instantiation (utility class)
    private ArrayCombination() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("This is a utility class and cannot be instantiated");
    }

    /**
     * Generates all possible combinations of length k from the integers 0 to n-1.
     * 
     * <p>The combinations are generated in lexicographical order (the order of the elements
     * in each combination is non-decreasing, and combinations are ordered by the first
     * element, then second, etc.).</p>
     *
     * @param totalElements The total number of elements (0, 1, 2, ..., totalElements-1)
     * @param combinationSize The desired length of each combination (k)
     * @return A list containing all valid combinations of size k
     * @throws IllegalArgumentException if totalElements or combinationSize are negative,
     *         or if combinationSize is greater than totalElements
     * 
     * @example
     * combination(4, 2) → [[0,1], [0,2], [0,3], [1,2], [1,3], [2,3]]
     * combination(3, 3) → [[0,1,2]]
     * combination(5, 1) → [[0], [1], [2], [3], [4]]
     * combination(3, 0) → [[]]
     */
    public static List<List<Integer>> findCombinations(int totalElements, int combinationSize) {
        // Validate input parameters
        if (combinationSize < 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Combination size cannot be negative: " + combinationSize);
        }
        if (totalElements < 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Total elements cannot be negative: " + totalElements);
        }
        if (combinationSize > totalElements) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "Combination size (" + combinationSize + ") cannot exceed total elements (" + totalElements + ")"
            );
        }

        // Special case: combination of size 0 (empty set)
        if (combinationSize == 0) {
            List<List<Integer>> emptyResult = new ArrayList<>();
            emptyResult.add(new ArrayList<>());
            return emptyResult;
        }

        // Special case: combination of size equal to total elements (only one combination)
        if (combinationSize == totalElements) {
            List<List<Integer>> fullSetResult = new ArrayList<>();
            List<Integer> fullCombination = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int elementIndex = 0; elementIndex < totalElements; elementIndex++) {
                fullCombination.add(elementIndex);
            }
            fullSetResult.add(fullCombination);
            return fullSetResult;
        }

        // Generate all combinations using backtracking
        List<List<Integer>> allCombinations = new ArrayList<>();
        generateCombinations(allCombinations, new ArrayList<>(), 0, totalElements, combinationSize);
        return allCombinations;
    }

    /**
     * Recursive helper method that uses backtracking to find all combinations.
     * 
     * <p>This method implements the core backtracking algorithm:
     * <ul>
     *   <li>Base case: When current combination reaches size k, add it to results</li>
     *   <li>Recursive case: Try each possible next element from start to n-1</li>
     *   <li>Backtrack: Remove the last element after exploring all possibilities with it</li>
     * </ul>
     * </p>
     *
     * @param allCombinations The list to store all valid combinations found
     * @param currentCombination The combination currently being built
     * @param startIndex The smallest number that can be added next (ensures non-decreasing order)
     * @param totalElements The total number of elements in the set (n)
     * @param targetSize The desired length of each combination (k)
     */
    private static void generateCombinations(List<List<Integer>> allCombinations, 
                                             List<Integer> currentCombination, 
                                             int startIndex, 
                                             int totalElements, 
                                             int targetSize) {

        // Base case: The current combination has reached the desired size
        if (currentCombination.size() == targetSize) {
            // Add a copy of the current combination to results
            allCombinations.add(new ArrayList<>(currentCombination));
            return;
        }

        // Try all possible next elements
        // Optimization: We only need to go up to totalElements - (targetSize - currentCombination.size())
        // because we need enough remaining elements to complete the combination
        int maxStartIndex = totalElements - (targetSize - currentCombination.size());

        for (int elementIndex = startIndex; elementIndex <= maxStartIndex; elementIndex++) {
            // Choose: Add element to current combination
            currentCombination.add(elementIndex);

            // Explore: Recursively build the rest of the combination
            generateCombinations(allCombinations, currentCombination, elementIndex + 1, totalElements, targetSize);

            // Unchoose: Backtrack - remove the last element to try other possibilities
            currentCombination.remove(currentCombination.size() - 1);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Returns the total number of possible combinations (binomial coefficient C(n,k)).
     * This method provides a way to check the expected result size.
     *
     * @param totalElements The total number of elements (n)
     * @param combinationSize The size of each combination (k)
     * @return The number of possible combinations (C(n,k))
     * @throws IllegalArgumentException if k > n or any parameter is negative
     */
    public static long countCombinations(int totalElements, int combinationSize) {
        if (combinationSize < 0 || totalElements < 0 || combinationSize > totalElements) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid parameters for counting combinations");
        }

        // Use symmetry: C(n,k) = C(n, n-k) to minimize computation
        int k = Math.min(combinationSize, totalElements - combinationSize);

        long result = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i <= k; i++) {
            result = result * (totalElements - k + i) / i;
        }
        return result;
    }

    /**
     * Prints all combinations in a readable format.
     * Useful for debugging and demonstration purposes.
     *
     * @param combinations The list of combinations to print
     */
    public static void printCombinations(List<List<Integer>> combinations) {
        if (combinations == null || combinations.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("No combinations found.");
            return;
        }

        System.out.println("Total combinations: " + combinations.size());
        for (int combinationIndex = 0; combinationIndex < combinations.size(); combinationIndex++) {
            System.out.printf("Combination %3d: %s%n", combinationIndex + 1, combinations.get(combinationIndex));
        }
    }
}

/*
COMPREHENSIVE DOCUMENTATION
===========================

COMBINATORICS BASICS
--------------------

What is a Combination?
- A combination is a selection of items from a set where order does NOT matter
- Example: {1,2,3} is the same combination as {3,2,1}
- Different from permutations where order matters

Mathematical Formula:
C(n,k) = n! / (k! × (n-k)!)

Where:
- n = total number of elements
- k = number of elements to choose
- ! = factorial (n! = n × (n-1) × ... × 2 × 1)

Examples:
---------
C(4,2) = 6 combinations: {0,1}, {0,2}, {0,3}, {1,2}, {1,3}, {2,3}
C(5,3) = 10 combinations
C(10,5) = 252 combinations

BACKTRACKING ALGORITHM DETAILS
------------------------------

Algorithm Visualization (n=4, k=2):
===================================

                    start()
                       |
                choose from [0,1,2,3]
                       |
        +--------------+--------------+--------------+
        |              |              |              |
       0(0)           1(1)           2(2)           3(3)
        |              |              |              |
    choose from     choose from     choose from     (cannot choose more -
     [1,2,3]         [2,3]           [3]             need k=2)
        |              |              |
    [0,1]✓          [1,2]✓          [2,3]✓
    [0,2]✓          [1,3]✓
    [0,3]✓

✓ = valid combination

RECURSION TREE EXPLANATION
--------------------------

Level 0: Empty combination []
Level 1: Choose first element (0, 1, 2, or 3)
Level 2: Choose second element (must be > first element)
Level 3: Reached k=2 → record combination and backtrack

PRUNING OPTIMIZATION
--------------------

The optimization in the for loop:
int maxStartIndex = n - (k - current.size())

Why? 
- We need (k - current.size()) more elements
- If we start too late, there won't be enough remaining numbers
- Example: n=5, k=3, current.size()=1
  - Need 2 more elements
  - maxStartIndex = 5 - 2 = 3
  - So we only consider elements 0,1,2,3 (not 4)
  - If we start at 4, we can't get 3 total elements

APPLICATIONS IN PROBLEM SOLVING
===============================

1. Lottery Number Generation
   - Generate all possible lottery ticket combinations
   - Calculate number of possible tickets

2. Subset Problems
   - Find all subsets of a given size
   - Used in power set generation algorithms

3. Team Formation
   - Generate all possible teams of size k from n players
   - Find optimal team combinations

4. Test Case Generation
   - Create all combinations of test parameters
   - Useful for combinatorial testing

5. Cryptography
   - Generating all possible key combinations
   - Brute-force attack simulation

6. Data Analysis
   - Feature selection combinations
   - Hyperparameter tuning

TIME COMPLEXITY ANALYSIS
=======================

| n | k | C(n,k) | Approximate Recursive Calls | Time (ms for 1M ops) |
|---|---|--------|----------------------------|---------------------|
| 5 | 2 | 10 | 30-40 | < 1 ms |
| 10 | 5 | 252 | ~1000 | ~1 ms |
| 20 | 10 | 184,756 | ~1M | ~1000 ms |
| 30 | 15 | 155,117,520 | ~1B | > 1 hour |

SPACE COMPLEXITY EXPLANATION
============================

Components:
1. Recursion Stack: O(k) - depth equals combination size
2. Current combination list: O(k)
3. Result storage: O(C(n,k) × k) - all combinations stored

Memory Usage Example (n=20, k=10):
- Number of combinations: 184,756
- Each combination: 10 integers × 8 bytes = 80 bytes
- Total memory: ~14.7 MB for results + overhead ~ 20-30 MB

NUMERICAL EXAMPLES
==================

Example 1: n=3, k=2
Input: combination(3, 2)
Process:
- Choose 0: then can choose 1,2 → [0,1], [0,2]
- Choose 1: then can choose 2 → [1,2]
- Choose 2: need 1 more but no elements left
Output: [[0,1], [0,2], [1,2]]

Example 2: n=4, k=3
Input: combination(4, 3)
Process:
- Choose 0: need 2 more → choose 1,2 → [0,1,2], choose 1,3 → [0,1,3], choose 2,3 → [0,2,3]
- Choose 1: need 2 more → choose 2,3 → [1,2,3]
Output: [[0,1,2], [0,1,3], [0,2,3], [1,2,3]]

RELATED ALGORITHMS
==================

1. Permutations (order matters) - more combinations
2. Subsets (all sizes) - includes combinations of all k
3. Combinations with repetition - elements can repeat
4. Power set - all subsets including empty set
5. Cartesian product - combinations from multiple sets
*/

